--TEST--
`Core\Test::assertThrows`'s second argument is a `C::class` written at the call, and `C` is a class of the `Throwable` tree. A string, a variable and a class outside the tree do not compile — `rule:testing/assertions-are-typed`
--FILE--
<?nvs
final class Order {}

interface Retryable {}

final class Busy extends RuntimeError implements Retryable {}

callable(): mixed $body = fn (): void => { throw new Busy("busy"); };
string $name = "RuntimeError";

// Refused: a string is checked only when the test runs.
Core\Test::assertThrows($body, "RuntimeError");
// Refused: a variable is the same.
Core\Test::assertThrows($body, $name);
// Refused: by name too.
Core\Test::assertThrows($body, expected: "RuntimeError");
// Refused: `Order` does not extend `Throwable`, so nothing throws it.
Core\Test::assertThrows($body, Order::class);
// Refused: an interface is not a class of the tree.
Core\Test::assertThrows($body, Retryable::class);

// Accepted: a class of the tree, its own or built in.
Core\Test::assertThrows($body, Busy::class);
Core\Test::assertThrows($body, RuntimeError::class);
Core\Test::assertThrows($body, expected: Throwable::class);
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0865]: `Core\Test::assertThrows` needs the error class written as `C::class`
%A
error[E0865]: `Core\Test::assertThrows` needs the error class written as `C::class`
%A
error[E0865]: `Core\Test::assertThrows` needs the error class written as `C::class`
%A
error[E0865]: `Order` is not an error class, so it is never thrown
%A
error[E0865]: `Retryable` is not an error class, so it is never thrown
%A
error: aborting due to 5 errors
